    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 3462.8,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 767,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to understand water truly, we have to look at it through a few different lenses: its unique physical properties, its role on Earth, and its importance to life.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Physical Properties of Water\nWater is famous among scientists for being weird. It defies many standard rules of chemistry and physics, which is precisely why it is able to support life.\n\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (oceans, rivers, rain), **solid** (ice, snow, glaciers), and **gas** (steam, water vapor, humidity).\n* **The Universal Solvent:** Water is exceptionally good at dissolving other substances. Because of its chemical structure (it has a slight positive charge on one end and a slight negative charge on the other), it can pull apart molecules of salt, sugar, minerals, and nutrients. This is how it transports nutrients through our bodies and minerals through the soil.\n* **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice cubes float in a drink. In nature, this is vital: when lakes and oceans freeze, ice floats to the top, creating an insulating layer that allows marine life to survive the winter underneath.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a massive amount of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet (oceans absorb heat from the sun and release it slowly) and helps regulate body temperature in living organisms.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nEarth is often called the \"Blue Planet\" because water covers about **71% of its surface**. \n* **Oceans:** About **96.5%** of all Earth's water is held in the oceans as saltwater. \n* **Freshwater:** Only about **2.5%** of Earth's water is fresh. And of that small amount, over two-thirds is locked away in glaciers and polar ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible in lakes, rivers, and the atmosphere for human use.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water on Earth is constantly moving in a closed system driven by the sun. It evaporates from oceans and lakes into the air, condenses into clouds, falls back to Earth as precipitation (rain or snow), and flows back to the oceans via rivers and groundwater. The water you drink today is the same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago!\n\n---\n\n### 3. Water and Life\nLife as we know it is entirely dependent on water. \n* **Biology:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. The brain is about 73% water, the lungs about 83%, and even bones are about 31% water. Water is the medium in which all the chemical reactions of life take place—digestion, blood circulation, temperature regulation, and waste removal.\n* **Ecosystems:** Water shapes landscapes through erosion and deposition, and it provides habitats for millions of species of plants and animals. \n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It is the lifeblood of our planet, a chemical marvel, and the fundamental requirement for the existence of biological life."
